<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norfolk Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> Island is a small island in the South Pacific between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and New Caledonia, now an external territory of Australia. Settled by the British in 1788, it served as a harsh penal colony until 1855. In 1856 the whole population of Pitcairn Island, descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">Bounty</a> mutineers, was moved there, and many of today's islanders descend from them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norfolk-island.jpg" alt="Norfolk Island" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Norfolk_Island.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Norfolk Island lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and New Caledonia, 1,412 kilometres directly east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s Evans Head and about 900 kilometres from Lord Howe Island. It covers about 35 square kilometres, with 32 kilometres of coast and no large internal bodies of water. Together with the neighbouring Phillip Island and Nepean Island it forms the Territory of Norfolk Island. The climate is humid subtropical, with warm summers and mild, rainy winters. The evergreen Norfolk Island pine is native to the island and appears on its flag.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological investigation suggests that East Polynesian seafarers settled the island in the 13th or 14th century, either from the Kermadec Islands or from the North Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Both Polynesian and Melanesian artefacts have been found. Occupation had ceased at least a few hundred years before Europeans arrived, probably because the island was isolated and poor for horticulture. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>, on his second voyage, was the first European known to have landed, on 10 October 1774, and named it after Mary Howard, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>.</p><p>The island served as a convict settlement from 6 March 1788 until 5 May 1855, except for an interval between 15 February 1814 and 6 June 1825, when it lay abandoned. In 1824 the British government instructed the Governor of New South Wales, Thomas Brisbane, to reoccupy it as a place for what it called the worst description of convicts. The landing of 6 June 1825 brought 104 people, of whom 57 were convicts.</p><p>On 8 June 1856 permanent civilian settlement began, when 194 descendants of Tahitians and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">HMS Bounty</a> mutineers, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fletcher-christian">Fletcher Christian</a>, arrived from the Pitcairn Islands, which had become too small for their growing number. They occupied many of the buildings left by the penal settlements and established farming and whaling. Some families returned to Pitcairn in 1858 and 1863. Norfolk Island was part of New South Wales from 1788 to 1844, then of Van Diemen's Land, and was transferred from the United Kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1914.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Norfolk Island is an external territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, with its capital at Kingston and its largest town at Burnt Pine. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 2,188 inhabitants. About half of the islanders trace their roots to Pitcairn Island. English and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> called Norfuk, a blend of 18th-century English and Tahitian based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitkern">Pitkern</a>, are both spoken. Tahitian influence survives in the hula dance and in dishes such as mudda, green banana dumplings. Tourism is the main economic activity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norfolk Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The whole population moved to <b>Norfolk Island</b> in 1856, but some families returned from 1858.</blockquote><a href="/state/pitcairn-islands">Pitcairn Islands</a></li></ul></section>
